## JWT Expiry Check Bug

The most common cause is a **units mismatch**. JWT `exp` is in **seconds**, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.

### The Broken Code

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - comparing milliseconds to seconds
const isExpired = Date.now() > payload.exp;

// Example: Date.now() = 1700000000000 (milliseconds)
// payload.exp = 1700000100 (seconds)
// 1700000000000 > 1700000100 → always true... or always false
// depending on token age, this comparison is meaningless
```

This means tokens will appear **never expired** because `Date.now()` (13 digits) is always astronomically larger than `exp` (10 digits)... 

Wait — actually that means it's **always expired**, so if tokens are getting *through*, there's likely a **logic error on top of the units bug**.

---

### Common Combined Mistake

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - units bug + inverted logic
function isTokenValid(payload) {
  if (Date.now() > payload.exp) {  // always true due to units bug
    return false;                   // always returns false (invalid)
  }
  return true;
}

// Then in middleware:
if (!isTokenValid(payload)) {
  // This block is always hit... 
}

// OR the check is simply skipped/short-circuited elsewhere
```

---

### The Fix

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT - convert exp to milliseconds before comparing
function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
  }

  let payload;
  try {
    // Option 1: Let your JWT library handle expiry (RECOMMENDED)
    payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET); 
    // jwt.verify() throws TokenExpiredError automatically

  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Auth error' });
  }

  req.user = payload;
  next();
}
```

```javascript
// Option 2: Manual check (if you must do it yourself)
function isTokenExpired(payload) {
  const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000); // ← key fix
  return nowInSeconds > payload.exp;
}

function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
  }

  let payload;
  try {
    // Decode without verifying expiry first
    payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, { 
      ignoreExpiration: true  // we'll check manually
    });
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
  }

  if (isTokenExpired(payload)) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
  }

  req.user = payload;
  next();
}
```

---

### Other Things to Check

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|---------|-----|
| Units mismatch (`Date.now()` vs `exp`) | Expiry never triggers | Divide `Date.now()` by 1000 |
| Inverted condition (`<` instead of `>`) | Expired tokens pass, valid ones fail | Fix comparison direction |
| `exp` field missing from token | Check always passes | Ensure token is signed with `expiresIn` |
| Middleware not applied to routes | Routes unprotected | Apply middleware correctly |
| `jwt.decode()` instead of `jwt.verify()` | Signature/expiry not checked | Use `jwt.verify()` |

---

### Verify Your Tokens Have `exp` Set

```javascript
// When signing, ensure expiresIn is set
const token = jwt.sign(
  { userId: user.id },
  process.env.JWT_SECRET,
  { expiresIn: '1h' }  // ← this adds the exp field
);

// Decode a token to inspect it
const decoded = jwt.decode(token);
console.log(decoded.exp);           // e.g., 1700003600 (seconds)
console.log(new Date(decoded.exp * 1000)); // human-readable expiry
```

**Bottom line:** Use `jwt.verify()` and let the library handle expiry — it's less error-prone than manual checks.
